    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\frac12,2,4$.",
      "worked": "The roots are $\\alpha,\\beta,\\alpha\\beta$. Their sums and products give\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\alpha+\\beta+\\alpha\\beta&=\\frac{13}{2}\\\\\n\\alpha\\beta+\\alpha^2\\beta+\\alpha\\beta^2&=11\\\\\n\\alpha\\beta(1+\\alpha+\\beta)&=11\\\\\n\\alpha^2\\beta^2&=4\\\\\n\\alpha\\beta&=\\pm2.\n\\end{align*}$$\nConsider $\\alpha\\beta=2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\alpha+\\beta+2&=\\frac{13}{2}\\\\\n\\alpha+\\beta&=\\frac92.\n\\end{align*}$$\nCheck the sum of products:\n$$2\\left(1+\\frac92\\right)=11\\quad\\checkmark$$\nIf $\\alpha\\beta=-2$, the sum of roots gives $\\alpha+\\beta=\\frac{17}{2}$, but\n$$-2\\left(1+\\frac{17}{2}\\right)=-19\\ne11.$$\nReject $\\alpha\\beta=-2$. Thus $\\alpha+\\beta=\\frac92$ and $\\alpha\\beta=2$. The two roots $\\alpha,\\beta$ satisfy\n$$\\begin{align*}\nt^2-\\frac92t+2&=0\\\\\n2t^2-9t+4&=0\\\\\n(2t-1)(t-4)&=0.\n\\end{align*}$$\nHence $\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}=\\{\\frac12,4\\}$ and $\\alpha\\beta=2$. The zeroes are $\\frac12,2,4$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
